border-image as a shorthand over its five longhands (source, slice, width, outset, repeat), so a rule that sets one
longhand (a pressed button's border-image-source) cascades over another rule's shorthand as CSS requires. The
shorthand reads the source and slices, widths and outsets after slashes, then the repeat, resetting omitted ones;
the longhands hold the canonical four-value boxes generation writes, composed back into one border-image text.
CSS Backgrounds 3: https://www.w3.org/TR/css-backgrounds-3/#border-image

